 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Honestly, I think you may be overreacting a bit on this.  Yes, they are sending your "digital ID" to their server to make sure that you're a licensed owner of the software.  But I don't see anywhere in there that says they're sending personal information.  <br>
<br>
From my understanding, what the digital ID does is that it grabs some numbers from your system (perhaps a serial number from the processor and/or montherboard?  something to that effect), and crunches them together with the actual product ID to get a unique identifier for your system.  It's just there to make sure that if you install a product with serial number 12345 on a system with ID 67890, that they don't end up getting calls from people with digital ID's of 12345666777, 12345999000, etc.  <br>
<br>
It really is kind of a smart way to do it, and if they do it like I described, there's not reason for them to gather any kind of personal information, or any info that would identify you.  Which isn't really that big a deal anyway, since most legitimate users will fill out the online registration anyway, so that they get their product support.<br>
<br>
Anyway, I guess my take is that IF MS is really grabbing personal info, or anything that could specifically identify ME just based on what software I'm running, or if they're gathering some sort of statistics or something, then yeah...they need to knock that djoy off.  But if they're just reading a few numbers from my hardware and crunching them into a unique identifier (like a hashing function), then I've got no problem with it.<br>
