 <FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" ><B></b></font>
<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Notice they are also using your digital ID number to diagnose possible hardware problems. That must mean that they can take that number and decode it to figure out exactly what is in your system. Do they really need to know that?<br>
<br>
Also, the pop-up window states<br>
<br>
<blockquote><FONT face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="1" >quote:</font><hr>"We do not intentionally collect your files, names, address, email address or any other form of personally identifiable information. However, the error report could contain customer- specific information such as data from open files. While this information could potentially be used to determine your identity, if present, it will not be used."<hr></blockquote><br>
So there will be some extra information being sent along "accidently" and stored in their database. This information could potentially be private. I don't want there to be ANY chance of my private information ending up in their database. They say they will not be using that info for anything, BUT Microsoft's servers have been hacked before... Not to mention corrupt employees could stumble accross info they shouldn't have.<br>
<br>
You being a libertarian party member, of all people, should see why this is bad. Microsoft might as well be the government of the internet... you would be up in arms if the U.S. government wanted to keep a database of YOUR system info, along with your IP address, serial number (which can be tied to your registration info... I.E. name and address), even if they stated it was only going to be used to troubleshoot US .gov websites or something.<br>
<br>
Ontop of the privacy issues... Your hardware ID will be checked online at a regular interval. Microsoft has stated that if your system is upgraded significantly, your software ID will of course change, leaving you unable to boot into windows until you get ahold of a Microsoft rep prove your identity and get a new key. This is going to prove a PAIN for people who upgrade a lot. It's gonna make things impossible for sites that do hardware review.<br>
<br>
What about reselling or transfering the license? If I buy a new computer, and, say it comes with say WinXP standard, but I already own WinXP Pro. I want to format the old computer and use WinXP Pro on the new one... do I have to buy a new copy of WinXP Pro? If I do buy a new copy of WinXP pro and sell my old system, along with the old version of WinXP pro, am I going to be able to transfer my license to the other person? If not... well, I can give them the comp, and it will work... but the second they upgrade enough to invalidate the Hardware ID, how are they supposed to reactivate it?<br>
<br>
I just don't think this is the answer to copy protection, especially since the software pirates will find a way around this online registration anyway. So... the people that will actually be in the database will be mainly legitimate customers whose lives have been made more difficult on behalf of software piracy.<br>
<br>
