<p><FONT face="verdana, tahoma" size="2" >Well, I musta glossed over the part about the "additional info" being sent.  That's not too cool.  <br>
<br>
But actually, as an LP member, I support MS's right to ensure that people don't rip them off, and I support MY right to deny them anything I don't want them to have.<br>
<br>
The unfortunate part about that is that in order to determine what exactly MS is going to get from me if I install their software, I basically have to be Mr. Lawyer and read all the fine print.  But is that really so bad?  How hard is it to actually READ the EULA before you agree to it?  I'm sure anything they're doing is in there.  MS is nothing if not paranoid.  They wouldn't do all sorts of stuff to your computer without at least giving them some sort of blanket power to do so.  And if you read the EULA, you should be able to spot language like "I grant MS the right to scan my memory, hard drive, and all porn directories, etc. etc."  If MS is doing this kind of stuff, then the best defense we have against it is to not buy their products.  Instead of IE6, use Opera or Netscape.  Instead of Windows, use Linux or BeOS.  <br>
<br>
It's not a great choice since MS controls the market for computer software, but HOW do they control it?  Because everyone buys their products.  If everyone stopped buying their products, then there would be more people (and their money) supporting things like Linux, which would help make it more compatible and user friendly (and get other developers to develop for it).  <br>
<br>
So I guess what it boils down to is that it's fine for MS to do whatever they want.  The phrase "caveat emptor" has never been more true than it is in today's age of fine print and EULA's on everything down to your toothpaste.  People need to start reading them before they sign them (it only takes 10 minutes...they're easier to understand than you think...and you only have to do it once.  Just like sex...er..nevermind).  And people need to start speaking louder with their wallets instead of being lazy and just going with MS because that's what everybody else does.  I think MS has done some great stuff, and they've done some crappy stuff.  And I'm definitely going to be more wary of them with all of this digital ID stuff.  But I don't necessarily think that most normal paying customers have too much to worry about, either.<br>
